.TH HYPRSHUTDOWN 1 "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hyprshutdown \- graceful shutdown utility for Hyprland
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hyprshutdown
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hyprshutdown
is a graceful shutdown utility for the Hyprland Wayland compositor.
It opens a GUI dialog and gracefully asks running applications to exit,
then quits Hyprland.
.PP
This is the recommended way to exit Hyprland. Using other methods
(such as
.B dispatch exit
) will cause applications to be forcefully killed rather than
given the opportunity to exit cleanly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-vt " N"
Switch to virtual terminal
.I N
after Hyprland exits. Useful on NVIDIA + SDDM systems to prevent a black
screen or hang on logout.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show the shutdown UI without actually closing any applications or exiting
Hyprland.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exit
Close applications gracefully but do not exit Hyprland afterwards.
.TP
.BR \-\-top\-label ", " \-t " \fITEXT\fR"
Set custom text to display at the top of the shutdown dialog.
.TP
.BR \-\-post\-cmd ", " \-p " \fICOMMAND\fR"
Run
.I COMMAND
after Hyprland has exited. Can be used to power off or reboot the system.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-fork
Run in the foreground instead of daemonizing.
.TP
.B \-\-verbose
Enable debug logging.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Show a help message and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Log out of Hyprland:
.PP
.RS
.B hyprshutdown
.RE
.PP
Shut the system down after closing apps:
.PP
.RS
.B hyprshutdown \-t 'Shutting down...' \-\-post\-cmd 'shutdown \-P 0'
.RE
.PP
Reboot the system after closing apps:
.PP
.RS
.B hyprshutdown \-t 'Restarting...' \-\-post\-cmd 'reboot'
.RE
.PP
Preview the dialog without doing anything:
.PP
.RS
.B hyprshutdown \-\-dry\-run
.RE
.SH NVIDIA + SDDM USERS
If you experience a black screen or hang when logging out with an NVIDIA GPU
and SDDM display manager, use the
.B \-\-vt
flag to force a virtual terminal switch after exit:
.PP
.RS
.B hyprshutdown \-\-vt 2
.RE
.PP
SDDM typically runs on VT2; check your system configuration if a different
terminal is needed.
.PP
The VT switch is performed via
.BR chvt (1)
and requires passwordless sudo access for that command. To configure this,
run:
.PP
.RS
.EX
echo "$USER ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/chvt" | sudo tee /etc/sudoers.d/chvt
sudo chmod 440 /etc/sudoers.d/chvt
.EE
.RE
.PP
This is considered safe because
.B chvt
only switches virtual terminals and cannot be exploited for privilege
escalation.
